# app Rules

Follow these patterns for `app/` development in `Next.js` of `house-of-froning.vercel.app`.

## File Structure

### Route-Level Organization (`app/[orgSlug]/[route]/`)

Use Next.js route groups `(actions)`, `(components)`, `(hooks)` for co-located feature code:

```md
apps/dashboard/app/[orgSlug]/[route]/
├── page.tsx                    # Main page (minimal logic)
├── layout.tsx                  # Route layout (if needed)
├── loading.tsx                 # Cache data read (if needed)
├── (actions)/                  # Server actions (route group)
│   └── route-actions.ts        # Server actions for this route
├── (components)/               # UI components (route group)
│   ├── ComponentName.tsx       # Pure UI components
│   └── ComponentNameModal.tsx  # Modal components
├── (hooks)/                    # Custom hooks (route group)
│   └── use-feature-name.ts     # React Query hooks, custom hooks
├── (context)/                  # Context providers (route group, if needed)
│   └── FeatureContext.tsx      # Shared state for route
└── types/                      # Local types (use @boom/types for shared types)
    └── route-types.ts          # Route-specific type definitions
```

### Shared Code Organization (`lib/`)

```md
apps/dashboard/lib/
├── services/                   # Service layer (database operations)
│   └── [service-name]/
│       └── server/
│           └── service-name.ts # Prisma operations, marked "server-only"
├── api/                        # API utilities and legacy actions
│   └── [feature]/
│       └── feature-actions.ts  # Legacy server actions (migrate to route actions)
├── auth/                       # Authentication & authorization
│   ├── actions/                # Auth server actions
│   ├── components/             # Auth UI components
│   └── utils/                  # Auth utilities
├── hooks/                      # Shared React hooks
│   └── use-shared-hook.ts      # Reusable hooks across routes
├── routes.ts                   # Route definitions
└── constants.ts                 # Shared constants
```

### Key Patterns

- **Route groups** `(actions)`, `(components)`, `(hooks)` don't affect URLs - they're organizational
- **Co-locate** feature code with its route using route groups
- **Services** in `lib/services/` are generic wrappers, not business logic
- **Actions** in route `(actions)/` folders call services, handle validation
- **Hooks** in route `(hooks)/` folders wrap actions with React Query

## Type Management

### Type Definitions

- Place all types and interfaces in `types.ts` files
- Exception: Component Props can be defined with the component

### Shared Types

- **ALWAYS** use `@boom/types` for shared interfaces
- Import from `@boom/types` for API responses, common interfaces
- Add new shared types to `packages/types/index.ts`

### Local Types

- Use local `types/` directory only for feature-specific types
- Re-export shared types from `@boom/types` when needed
- Don't duplicate types that exist in `@boom/types`
- Local types are incredibly **limited use case(s)**

## API Route Deprecation

When possible, always defer to `server actions` and `services/server` for hitting backend routes. If unavoidable and refactoring legacy code, **ALWAYS** use standardized `ApiResponse` types:

```typescript
import {
  ApiResponse,
  createSuccessResponse,
  createErrorResponse,
} from "@boom/types";

export async function GET(): Promise<NextResponse<ApiSuccessResponse<Data[]>>> {
  try {
    const data = await Service.getData();
    return NextResponse.json(createSuccessResponse(data));
  } catch (error) {
    return NextResponse.json(createErrorResponse("Failed to fetch data"), {
      status: 500,
    });
  }
}
```

## Authentication Patterns

- **Client-side:** Browser handles cookies automatically
- **Server Actions:** Pass cookies explicitly with `cookieStore.toString()`
- **API Routes:** Use Supabase auth with proper error handling

## Migration Strategy

- **New development:** Always use React Query + Server Actions + Services
- **Existing code:** Refactor when touching for other reasons
- **API routes:** Migrate to `ApiResponse` types when updating
- **Action Guards:** Every `server action` is guarded by an `auth/util`
